---
/**
 * PrivacyArticle.astro — the single source for the /about/privacy prose.
 *
 * Rendered by both `src/pages/about/privacy.astro` (EN) and
 * `src/pages/[lang]/about/privacy.astro` (localized chrome, English prose)
 * so the two can no longer drift — they had: the localized copy predated
 * the server-side analytics section.
 *
 * Keep this page in step with what the code does:
 *   - Optional GA4: src/components/Analytics.astro, src/lib/analytics-client.ts
 *   - Property settings (retention, Signals): docs/GA4-SETUP.md
 * The page promises that material changes are visible in Git history.
 */
import { localePathFor } from '../lib/seo';

interface Props {
  lang: string;
}

const { lang } = Astro.props;
const updated = '2026-11-23';
const colophonHref = localePathFor('/about/colophon', lang);
---

<main id="main" class="reader">
  <article>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      sohamhamso is a static reader. We collect almost nothing by default.
      This page explains the two exceptions — opt-in email and opt-in
      analytics — and how to turn each off.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-collect">What we collect</h2>
    <p>
      Reading the site requires nothing. No account, no cookie wall, no
      tracker network. The pages you read are served as static HTML from
      the edge. Your IP address is visible to our hosting provider for
      the duration of each request, the same way every website on the
      public internet works, and it is not stored against your identity
      by us.
    </p>

    <p>
      The first exception is the daily-verse email. If you subscribe, we
      store an HMAC-SHA256 hash of your address — not the plaintext
      email — along with your language preference and an unsubscribe
      token. The hash means we can deliver mail and check duplicates
      without keeping a usable email list at rest. We do not share or
      sell anything.
    </p>

    <p>
      The second exception is Google Analytics, which runs only if you
      allow it. See <a href="#analytics">Optional Google Analytics</a>
      below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-dont">What we don't use</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No analytics scripts unless you opt in. Google Analytics loads only
        after you choose “Allow analytics”. No Plausible, no Fathom.
      </li>
      <li>No advertising or remarketing networks.</li>
      <li>No third-party social embeds.</li>
      <li>No fingerprinting, session replay, or A/B testing tools.</li>
      <li>
        No cookies unless you allow analytics. Your reading settings (theme,
        script, languages) are kept in your browser's local storage; they
        are never sent to us, and reach Google only as described below if
        you allow analytics.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="analytics">Optional Google Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      On your first visit a small banner asks whether we may count your
      visit with Google Analytics 4. Until you choose “Allow analytics”,
      no Google script is loaded and no analytics cookie is set. If your
      browser sends a Global Privacy Control signal, we treat that as
      “no” and do not ask.
    </p>
    <p>If you allow it, Google Analytics receives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        the pages you open (including the words you type into site
        search, which appear in the search page's address), the page that
        referred you, how long a page stays in view, and whether you
        scroll to the end of it;
      </li>
      <li>links you follow off the site and files you download from it;</li>
      <li>
        your browser, device type, screen size, and an approximate
        location that Google derives from your IP address;
      </li>
      <li>
        your reading choices on this site: interface language, reader
        language, script, and which parallel translations you open;
      </li>
      <li>
        the fact that you submitted the daily-verse form, with the
        delivery language — never your email address.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It sets first-party cookies named <code>_ga</code> and
      <code>_ga_…</code> to recognise a returning browser. Google Signals
      and ad personalization are turned off, and the data is not linked
      to any advertising account. Event-level data is kept for two
      months, the shortest retention Google Analytics offers; reports
      keep aggregate counts. We use it to decide which texts and
      languages to translate next and to see where readers come from.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can change your mind at any time: open the reading settings
      (the <strong>Aa</strong> button) and clear “Allow Google Analytics”.
      That stops collection and deletes the analytics cookies from this
      site. The email confirmation and unsubscribe pages never load
      analytics. Google processes this data under its own
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="server-side-analytics">Server-side request data</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on Cloudflare, which proxies every request
      to our edge. As a side effect of routing, Cloudflare sees the
      same request metadata any web host sees: source IP, requested
      URL, user agent, response status. We use Cloudflare's free
      zone-level <em>Web Analytics</em> view of that data
      (aggregate pageviews and unique visitors) so we can tell
      whether the site is up and which pages are read. This is
      server-side observation: Cloudflare delivers no client-side
      beacon script, sets no cookies, and does not associate requests
      with any persistent visitor identifier.
    </p>
    <p>
      During the first 72 hours after launch (2026-06-08 →
      2026-06-11), Cloudflare also writes request logs to a private
      backup bucket so we can spot broken links and server errors.
      That window is single-purpose and ends with the logs being
      purged. See <code>docs/CF-OBSERVABILITY-SETUP.md</code> in
      the public repo for the operator runbook.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unsubscribe">Unsubscribe</h2>
    <p>
      Every daily-verse email includes a one-click unsubscribe link
      tied to the token mentioned above. Following it deletes your row
      from the subscribers database. If you want a manual deletion
      instead, write to the address listed on the
      <a href={colophonHref}>colophon</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this page changes materially, the change is recorded in the
      public Git history of the site. The page is generated from a
      single source file, not a CMS, so there is no hidden version.
    </p>

    <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}.</p>
  </article>
</main>

<style>
  .reader {
    max-width: 65ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--spacing-7) var(--spacing-4) var(--spacing-9);
    font-family: var(--font-iast);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .reader {
      padding: var(--spacing-9) var(--spacing-6) var(--spacing-10);
    }
  }

  h1 {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 32px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-3);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    h1 { font-size: 40px; }
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-7);
  }

  h2 {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin: var(--spacing-7) 0 var(--spacing-3);
  }

  p, li {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25em;
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-4);
  }

  li { margin-bottom: var(--spacing-2); }

  .updated {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-7);
    font-family: var(--font-chrome);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }
</style>
